{
 "id": "E09",
 "title": "Rookie's opponents want the net as pro's do, with slower hands: they shoot from the slot with a wind-up and lift a stick now and then",
 "date": "2026-11-25",
 "pillar": "P6 a level is harder by better play (R-0067: 'a level is easy by clumsiness, never by absence. Rookie's bots want the puck and the net as pro's do; they are slower to release, wider in aim, later to read')",
 "targets": [
  "T8"
 ],
 "hypothesis": "At rookie every setting of the planning hand wins 100 % and a hand that never moves wins 62 %: rookie is won without the player. Rookie's opponents already skate at the human's pace (hk_opp_pace9 1.0; their mean speed in play is 0.90 of his because they play less), read late (24 frames), aim 6 degrees wide and poke after 14 frames. What they lack is absence, not clumsiness: they shoot only from inside 11.5 m and tap it (hk_shoot_extra9_m 10, no wind-up), and they never lift a stick (lift 0), so they neither score nor win pucks. The change, rookie only: they shoot from inside 17.5 m (hk_shoot_extra9_m 16) with a short wind-up (hk_shot_charge9_frames 8; still 6 degrees wide), and they lift a stick with a long rest between (lift 1, lift_cooldown_frames 150; pro 90). Expected: the idle hand's wins fall (62 % toward 40 %); the planning novice no longer wins every match (toward R-0029's 85 %), the regular and the good still win nearly all; the hands spread at rookie in wins or at least in goal difference.",
